.TH W 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
w \- show who is logged on and what they are doing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B w
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B w
first prints a header line in the same format as
.BR uptime (1):
the current time, the time since boot, the number of logged-in users
(all
.B USER_PROCESS
records in the utmp file), and the three load averages.
.PP
It then prints a table with one row per
.B USER_PROCESS
record:
.TP
.B USER
Login name.
.TP
.B TTY
Terminal line, relative to
.IR /dev .
.TP
.B FROM
Remote host recorded at login, or
.B \-
for a local login.
.TP
.B LOGIN@
Login time, as
.IR hh : mm
in local time.
.TP
.B WHAT
The command name of the foreground process on that terminal, or
.B \-
if none can be found.
.PP
The
.B WHAT
column is derived from
.IR /proc :
a process qualifies if its process group equals the terminal's
foreground process group (the
.I pgrp
and
.I tpgid
fields of
.IR /proc/pid/stat )
and one of its file descriptors 0, 1 or 2 is a link to
.IR /dev/line .
The process-group leader is preferred when several processes qualify.
.PP
Non-printable characters in the user, line and host fields are shown as
.BR ? .
.PP
.B w
takes no options; any arguments are ignored.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B w
always exits with status 0.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/run/utmp
Current login records.
.TP
.IR /proc/uptime ", " /proc/loadavg
Uptime and load averages.
.TP
.IR /proc/pid/stat ", " /proc/pid/fd/
Used to find the foreground command on each terminal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ps (1),
.BR top (1),
.BR uptime (1),
.BR who (1),
.BR proc (5),
.BR utmp (5)
